A database index helps the database find records faster without checking every row in a table. It works like the index of a book, helping us find information quickly.
MySQL commonly uses B-tree indexes to organise values and locate the required records.

Why would an index improve this query?
Since the query joins four large tables, an index on frequently used JOIN columns can help the database find matching records faster and reduce full table scans. This can improve query performance.

Can adding indexes hurt performance?
Yes. Too many indexes can create problems:
- INSERT: New records take longer to insert because indexes also need updating.
- UPDATE: Updating indexed columns may take longer.
- DELETE: Deleting records also requires index maintenance.
- Storage: Extra indexes use additional storage space.

What is the main trade-off?
The main trade-off is between read performance, write performance and storage space. Indexes can make data retrieval faster, but they can slow down data changes.
Therefore, I would add indexes only to useful columns and check the query execution plan instead of creating indexes on every column.

